<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>fundamentals-of-applied-chemistry</title></head><body><h1>fundamentals-of-applied-chemistry</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry: chemical industry and …</li><li>Stages in producing a new product</li><li>Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle</li><li>Continuous and batch processing</li><li>Environmental impact of the chemical industry</li></ul><h2>Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry: chemical industry and …</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Fact.** Force on moving charge F = q(v×B); on a wire F = BIL sinθ.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fact.** Faraday: EMF ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz's law gives the sign (induced current opposes the change in flux).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Self-inductance L = NΦ/I; inductance stores energy U = ½LI². Mutual inductance M couples two coils.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Chemical industry: fertilizers, pharmaceuticals, dyes.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Fuel chemistry: combustion, engines.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Water treatment: purification, desalination.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Materials science: ceramics, composites.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry: chemical industry</td></tr><tr><td>• Its importance</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ All cells look the same under a microscope. ✅ Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles; eukaryotic cells have them.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ DNA and RNA are identical in structure. ✅ DNA is double-stranded with deoxyribose and thymine; RNA is single-stranded with ribose and uracil.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Mitosis produces genetically different cells. ✅ Mitosis produces genetically identical diploid daughter cells.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Meiosis produces identical cells. ✅ Meiosis produces 4 genetically diverse haploid gametes.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Crossing over occurs in mitosis. ✅ Crossing over occurs in Prophase I of meiosis only.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All enzymes are proteins. ✅ Most enzymes are proteins; some RNA molecules (ribozymes) also have catalytic activity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ ATP is the only energy currency. ✅ GTP, UTP, and CTP also serve as energy carriers in specific pathways.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Photosynthesis occurs in all plant cells. ✅ Photosynthesis occurs only in cells containing chloroplasts (mainly mesophyll).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Respiration and photosynthesis are opposite processes with same equation. ✅ They are complementary but occur in different organelles and under different conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Oxygen is produced during respiration. ✅ Oxygen is consumed in aerobic respiration; produced during photosynthesis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Food chain shows all feeding relationships. ✅ Food chain is linear; food web shows interconnected feeding relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 90% of energy is transferred to next trophic level. ✅ Only ~10% energy transfers; ~90% lost as heat.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Biomagnification decreases toxin concentration up the food chain. ✅ Biomagnification increases toxin concentration at higher trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Carbon cycle only involves plants and animals. ✅ Carbon cycle involves atmosphere, oceans, fossils, rocks, and decomposers.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Nitrogen fixation only occurs in bacteria. ✅ Some lightning and industrial processes (Haber) also fix nitrogen.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All fungi are harmful. ✅ Many fungi are beneficial (decomposers, mycorrhizae, food, antibiotics like penicillin).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Viruses are living organisms. ✅ Viruses are acellular — they cannot reproduce without a host and lack metabolism.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Bacteria are always harmful. ✅ Most bacteria are harmless or beneficial (gut flora, nitrogen fixation, decomposition).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gram-positive and Gram-negative differ only in color. ✅ Gram-positive has thick peptidoglycan layer; Gram-negative has thin layer + outer membrane.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Natural selection creates perfect organisms. ✅ Natural selection favors traits suited to current environment; environments change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Evolution is goal-oriented. ✅ Evolution has no goal; it acts on random variation filtered by selection.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Homologous and analogous structures mean the same thing. ✅ Homologous = common ancestry (divergent); Analogous = similar function, different origin (convergent).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Fossils are preserved bones only. ✅ Fossils include impressions, trace fossils, amber-preserved organisms, and molecular fossils.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Lamarckism is completely wrong. ✅ Lamarck's inheritance of acquired characteristics is largely rejected, but epigenetics shows some environmental effects can be inherited.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Five-kingdom classification is outdated. ✅ Six-kingdom (adding Archaea) and phylogenetic systems are more current, but five-kingdom remains useful for education.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Binomial nomenclature names change frequently. ✅ Names are stable once formally published; changes require formal revision by taxonomic authorities.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All algae are aquatic. ✅ Some algae live in soil, on tree bark, or in symbiotic relationships (lichens).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Bryophytes have vascular tissue. ✅ Bryophytes (mosses, liverworts, hornworts) lack true vascular tissue (xylem/phloem).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gymnosperms produce flowers. ✅ Gymnosperms have naked seeds in cones; angiosperms produce flowers and enclosed seeds.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Double fertilization occurs in gymnosperms. ✅ Double fertilization (zygote + endosperm) is unique to angiosperms.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Earthworms have an open circulatory system. ✅ Earthworms have a closed circulatory system with dorsal and ventral vessels.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Frogs breathe only through lungs. ✅ Frogs use cutaneous respiration (skin), buccal cavity, and lungs.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Conservation only means protecting animals. ✅ Conservation includes genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity; in-situ and ex-situ strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ In-situ and ex-situ conservation are the same. ✅ In-situ = conservation in natural habitat (national parks); Ex-situ = conservation outside habitat (zoos, seed banks).</td></tr></table><h2>Stages in producing a new product</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Chemical industry: fertilizers, pharmaceuticals, dyes.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Fuel chemistry: combustion, engines.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Water treatment: purification, desalination.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Materials science: ceramics, composites.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Stages in producing a new product</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ All cells look the same under a microscope. ✅ Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound organelles; eukaryotic cells have them.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ DNA and RNA are identical in structure. ✅ DNA is double-stranded with deoxyribose and thymine; RNA is single-stranded with ribose and uracil.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Mitosis produces genetically different cells. ✅ Mitosis produces genetically identical diploid daughter cells.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Meiosis produces identical cells. ✅ Meiosis produces 4 genetically diverse haploid gametes.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Crossing over occurs in mitosis. ✅ Crossing over occurs in Prophase I of meiosis only.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All enzymes are proteins. ✅ Most enzymes are proteins; some RNA molecules (ribozymes) also have catalytic activity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ ATP is the only energy currency. ✅ GTP, UTP, and CTP also serve as energy carriers in specific pathways.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Photosynthesis occurs in all plant cells. ✅ Photosynthesis occurs only in cells containing chloroplasts (mainly mesophyll).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Respiration and photosynthesis are opposite processes with same equation. ✅ They are complementary but occur in different organelles and under different conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Oxygen is produced during respiration. ✅ Oxygen is consumed in aerobic respiration; produced during photosynthesis.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Food chain shows all feeding relationships. ✅ Food chain is linear; food web shows interconnected feeding relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 90% of energy is transferred to next trophic level. ✅ Only ~10% energy transfers; ~90% lost as heat.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Biomagnification decreases toxin concentration up the food chain. ✅ Biomagnification increases toxin concentration at higher trophic levels.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Carbon cycle only involves plants and animals. ✅ Carbon cycle involves atmosphere, oceans, fossils, rocks, and decomposers.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Nitrogen fixation only occurs in bacteria. ✅ Some lightning and industrial processes (Haber) also fix nitrogen.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All fungi are harmful. ✅ Many fungi are beneficial (decomposers, mycorrhizae, food, antibiotics like penicillin).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Viruses are living organisms. ✅ Viruses are acellular — they cannot reproduce without a host and lack metabolism.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Bacteria are always harmful. ✅ Most bacteria are harmless or beneficial (gut flora, nitrogen fixation, decomposition).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gram-positive and Gram-negative differ only in color. ✅ Gram-positive has thick peptidoglycan layer; Gram-negative has thin layer + outer membrane.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Natural selection creates perfect organisms. ✅ Natural selection favors traits suited to current environment; environments change.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Evolution is goal-oriented. ✅ Evolution has no goal; it acts on random variation filtered by selection.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Homologous and analogous structures mean the same thing. ✅ Homologous = common ancestry (divergent); Analogous = similar function, different origin (convergent).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Fossils are preserved bones only. ✅ Fossils include impressions, trace fossils, amber-preserved organisms, and molecular fossils.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Lamarckism is completely wrong. ✅ Lamarck's inheritance of acquired characteristics is largely rejected, but epigenetics shows some environmental effects can be inherited.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Five-kingdom classification is outdated. ✅ Six-kingdom (adding Archaea) and phylogenetic systems are more current, but five-kingdom remains useful for education.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Binomial nomenclature names change frequently. ✅ Names are stable once formally published; changes require formal revision by taxonomic authorities.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All algae are aquatic. ✅ Some algae live in soil, on tree bark, or in symbiotic relationships (lichens).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Bryophytes have vascular tissue. ✅ Bryophytes (mosses, liverworts, hornworts) lack true vascular tissue (xylem/phloem).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Gymnosperms produce flowers. ✅ Gymnosperms have naked seeds in cones; angiosperms produce flowers and enclosed seeds.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Double fertilization occurs in gymnosperms. ✅ Double fertilization (zygote + endosperm) is unique to angiosperms.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Earthworms have an open circulatory system. ✅ Earthworms have a closed circulatory system with dorsal and ventral vessels.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Frogs breathe only through lungs. ✅ Frogs use cutaneous respiration (skin), buccal cavity, and lungs.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ Conservation only means protecting animals. ✅ Conservation includes genetic, species, and ecosystem diversity; in-situ and ex-situ strategies.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ In-situ and ex-situ conservation are the same. ✅ In-situ = conservation in natural habitat (national parks); Ex-situ = conservation outside habitat (zoos, seed banks).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ All plants perform photosynthesis. ✅ Some parasitic plants (e.g., dodder) lack chlorophyll and do not photosynthesize.</td></tr></table><h2>Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle — Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: economics of production, cash flow in the production cycle · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Cash flow in the production cycle.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Economics of production</td></tr><tr><td>• Cash flow in the production cycle</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Economics of production, cash flow in the production cycle. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Continuous and batch processing</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Continuous and batch processing — Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: continuous, batch processing · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Batch processing.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Continuous</td></tr><tr><td>• Batch processing</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Continuous and batch processing (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Continuous, batch processing. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Environmental impact of the chemical industry</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Environmental impact of the chemical industry — Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: environmental impact of the chemical industry · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Force on moving charge F = q(v×B); on a wire F = BIL sinθ.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Faraday: EMF ε = −dΦ/dt; Lenz's law gives the sign (induced current opposes the change in flux).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Self-inductance L = NΦ/I; inductance stores energy U = ½LI². Mutual inductance M couples two coils.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Environmental impact of the chemical industry</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Environmental impact of the chemical industry (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Environmental impact of the chemical industry. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry: chemical industry and …</td><td>**Fact.** Force on moving charge F = q(v×B); on a wire F = BIL sinθ.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Stages in producing a new product</td><td>Chemical industry: fertilizers, pharmaceuticals, dyes.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle</td><td>Economics of production; cash flow in the production cycle (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Economics of production, cash flow in the production cycle. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Continuous and batch processing</td><td>Continuous and batch processing (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Continuous, batch processing. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Environmental impact of the chemical industry</td><td>Environmental impact of the chemical industry (Fundamentals of Applied Chemistry, Chemistry, class-11-notes) covers Environmental impact of the chemical industry. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</td></tr></table><!-- academic-extension:start --><section class="academic-extension" aria-labelledby="academic-extension-title"><h2 id="academic-extension-title">Academic Extension: Evidence, Conditions &amp; Open Questions</h2><p><b>Topic bridge — Fundamentals Of Applied Chemistry.</b> Use this extension to connect the topic’s core material with deeper academic inquiry: state a claim, name the conditions under which it holds, and distinguish established knowledge from an open research or interpretive question.</p><h3>Established academic fact</h3><p>Chemical properties arise from the arrangement and interaction of electrons and nuclei. Conservation of atoms and charge constrains reactions; molecular structure and energy changes help explain which products form.</p><h3>Conditions, assumptions &amp; limits</h3><p>Treat oxidation state, bonding diagrams and reaction equations as models with defined rules. Temperature, pressure, solvent, concentration, particle size and catalysts can alter rates or equilibria; name the conditions whenever comparing results.</p><h3>Open research question — Circular materials and selective recycling</h3><p>Researchers are working to recover and reuse active battery materials at scale. Real waste streams vary in chemistry and condition, so sorting, safe disassembly, purity, energy use and economic viability all constrain the process.</p><h3>Inquiry prompt</h3><p>For “Fundamentals Of Applied Chemistry”, identify one assumption in the model, explain what observation would test it, and state what evidence could distinguish the accepted account from the open question “Circular materials and selective recycling”.</p><h3>Further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-1-chemical-equilibria">OpenStax Chemistry: chemical equilibrium</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-5-periodic-variations-in-element-properties">OpenStax Chemistry: periodic trends</a></li><li><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10480636/">Peer-reviewed review: direct recycling of lithium-ion batteries</a></li></ul><p><small>Curriculum note: the research question is an enrichment extension, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed NEB text and syllabus for examinable scope.</small></p></section><!-- academic-extension:end --></body></html>